.TH KARIDNS\-SIGNER 8 "2026-11-13" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/karidns-signer.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
karidns\-signer \- keeps the zones of a KariDNS database (PostgreSQL or SQLite3)
signed: static DNSSEC, signed again before the signatures run out and whenever a
zone changes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-signer [\-c <config>]
karidns\-signer [\-c <config>] \-\-check
karidns\-signer \-\-version | \-h | \-\-help
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBkaridns\-signer\fR (0.6.0, item C) is a Node.js daemon that
\fBorchestrates\fR the signing of the zones in a PowerDNS 5.1 database that
\fBkaridns(8)\fR serves through \fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR. It does no DNSSEC
arithmetic and does not touch the zone data itself:
.PP
.TS
lbw(35) lbw(36)
lw(35) lw(36).
\&Task	\&Done by
_
\&generate keys	\&BIND 9.20 \fBdnssec\-keygen\fR
T{
sign the zone (NSEC / NSEC3 chain, RRSIG, SOA serial)
T}	T{
BIND 9.20 \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR (its own verification step is \fBverify\fR)
T}
T{
read the zone, write the DNSSEC records back
T}	T{
\fBkaridns\-zone\fR (\fBexport \-\-signer\fR, \fBimport \-\-signer\fR)
T}
\&check the signed zone	\&\fBkaricheck zone\fR
\&ask the server to read the zone	T{
a row in \fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR (PostgreSQL) or \fBkarictl reload\fR
(SQLite3)
T}
.TE
.PP
What Node does itself: the schedule (\fBkaridns_signer_state\fR), the key
requests (\fBkaridns_key_requests\fR), the keys (\fBcryptokeys\fR rows to
\fBK*.private\fR / \fBK*.key\fR files and back, with \fBnode:crypto\fR for the
public half of a key), and the child processes. The only npm package is \fBpg\fR
(exact version, \fBpackage\-lock.json\fR, installed with \fBnpm ci
\-\-ignore\-scripts\fR); SQLite3 uses the \fBnode:sqlite\fR of Node. Node 22.13
or newer is needed (the package uses Node 24).
.PP
The signer serves the model of the rest of KariDNS: \fBstatic\fR DNSSEC. The
signed zone is data in the database, the server serves it as it is. There is no
online signing, \fBno automated key rollover\fR (the procedure is the
operator's, below), and no CDS / CDNSKEY publication; those belong to a later
release.
.PP
A zone is the signer's when it has the domain metadata \fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR
= \fB1\fR. The signer sets it itself: \fBa key request of kind \fBenable\fB is
how a zone is put under the signer\fR (below), or \fBkaridns\-zone import
\-\-signer\fR of a zone you signed yourself.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 8
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fIfile\fR
The configuration file. Default
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns\-signer.conf\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-check\fR
Read the configuration, check the permissions of the work directory and of the
\fBconninfo\-file\fR, that the SQLite3 file can be opened for writing, the
versions of \fBdnssec\-keygen\fR, \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR, \fBkaridns\-zone\fR
(and that it has the driver of \fBdatabase\fR), \fBkaricheck\fR and, with
SQLite3, \fBkarictl\fR; then exit. Every line is \fBok: ...\fR or
\fBproblem: ...\fR. Exit status 0 when fine, 1 when there is a problem. It does
not connect to the database.
.TP 8
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version.
.TP 8
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print the usage.
.PP
\fBSignals.\fR \fBSIGTERM\fR and \fBSIGINT\fR stop the daemon after the zones
that are being signed are finished (their key files are wiped), exit status 0; a
second signal does not interrupt a zone. \fBSIGHUP\fR reads the configuration
file again: a file with a mistake is refused and the old settings
stay. \fBdatabase\fR, \fBconninfo\-file\fR, \fBsqlite\-file\fR, \fBschema\fR and
\fBwork\-dir\fR need a restart (SIGHUP logs a warning and keeps the old value);
so do \fBlog\fR and the paths of the tools (\fBkaridns\-zone\fR,
\fBkaricheck\fR, \fBkarictl\fR, \fBbind\-tools\fR), which are read at start
only. Everything else (\fBworkers\fR, the signature times, \fBverify\fR,
\fBnsec3\-opt\-out\fR, the per-zone sections, \fBtimeout\fR, \fBdebounce\fR,
\fBkarictl\-config\fR, \fBkarictl\-socket\fR) is used from the next zone on.
.PP
\fBExit status.\fR 0 after \fBSIGTERM\fR / \fBSIGINT\fR, or for \fB\-\-help\fR,
\fB\-\-version\fR and a fine \fB\-\-check\fR; 1 for a configuration or database
problem, a fatal error while running (the connection to the database is lost and
cannot be made again) or a \fB\-\-check\fR problem; 2 for a usage error.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
Lines of \fBkey = value\fR; \fB#\fR starts a comment (in a value, after white
space); a value may be in double quotes; \fB[zone <name>]\fR starts the section
of one zone. A key written twice in one place, an unknown key and a bad value
are errors that name the line. Durations are a number with an optional unit
\fBs\fR, \fBm\fR, \fBh\fR, \fBd\fR or \fBw\fR (a bare number is
seconds). \fBkaridns\-signer.conf.sample\fR shows all of it.
.TP 8
\fBdatabase\fR
\fIDefault:\fR (required)
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBpg\fR or \fBsqlite3\fR.
.TP 8
\fBconninfo\-file\fR
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBpg\fR: the file with the connection string of the signer's
role. A regular file (no link) owned by the user the signer runs as, mode
\fB0600\fR or \fB0400\fR (checked like the loader checks its own). Keywords:
\fBhost\fR, \fBport\fR, \fBdbname\fR, \fBuser\fR, \fBpassword\fR, \fBsslmode\fR,
\fBconnect_timeout\fR, \fBapplication_name\fR; another keyword is refused. The
Node driver has no opportunistic TLS: \fBsslmode=disable\fR, \fBallow\fR,
\fBprefer\fR and no \fBsslmode\fR all mean plain text. For a host that is not
local (a Unix socket directory, \fBlocalhost\fR, \fB127.0.0.0/8\fR and \fB::1\fR
are local) without \fBrequire\fR, \fBverify\-ca\fR or \fBverify\-full\fR,
\fB\-\-check\fR prints \fBwarning: the connection to the database at HOST is not
encrypted (sslmode=X); use sslmode=verify\-full (require encrypts but does not
check the server)\fR and the start logs it; it is a warning, not a
refusal. \fBrequire\fR encrypts but does not check the server's certificate;
\fBverify\-full\fR does. \fBsslrootcert\fR is not read: a certificate from your
own CA needs that CA in the service's environment
(\fBNODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/path/to/ca.pem\fR).
.TP 8
\fBschema\fR
\fIDefault:\fR the role's \fBsearch_path\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBpg\fR: the schema of the PowerDNS and \fBkaridns_*\fR tables.
.TP 8
\fBsqlite\-file\fR
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBsqlite3\fR: the database file. The user needs write access to
the file \fBand its directory\fR (\fB<file>.signer.lock\fR is created there, WAL
files too). The file holds the private keys of the zones: when other users (not
the owner, not the group) can read it, \fB\-\-check\fR and the start warn
(\fBchmod o\-rwx\fR it).
.TP 8
\fBkarictl\-config\fR
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBsqlite3\fR: the \fBkarictl\fR configuration of the restricted
key that asks the server to reload a zone (below). Required for \fBsqlite3\fR.
.TP 8
\fBkarictl\-socket\fR
\fIDefault:\fR from that file
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBsqlite3\fR: the control socket (\fBkarictl \-s\fR).
.TP 8
\fBwork\-dir\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB/var/db/karidns\-signer/work\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Where the key files and the work files of a zone live while it is
signed: \fB<work\-dir>/<zone>.<pid>.<n>/\fR, mode \fB0700\fR, files
\fB0600\fR. It must be a directory of the signer's user with no access for group
or others (checked at start). Put it on tmpfs (the rc script does).
.TP 8
\fBworkers\fR
\fIDefault:\fR half of the CPUs, at least 1
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Zones signed at the same time (1 to 256).
.TP 8
\fBkaridns\-zone\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB/usr/local/bin/karidns\-zone\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Must have the driver of \fBdatabase\fR (\fBkaridns\-zone
\-\-version\fR lists them).
.TP 8
\fBkaricheck\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB/usr/local/bin/karicheck\fR
.TP 8
\fBkarictl\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB/usr/local/bin/karictl\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBsqlite3\fR only.
.TP 8
\fBbind\-tools\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB/usr/local/bin\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR The directory of \fBdnssec\-keygen\fR and \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR
(BIND 9.20, the FreeBSD package \fBbind\-tools\fR).
.TP 8
\fBvalidity\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB14d\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR How long a signature is valid (\fBdnssec\-signzone \-e
now+validity\fR). At least 60 s.
.TP 8
\fBrefresh\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB302400\fR (3.5 days)
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR A signature is made again when this much of its life is left.
.TP 8
\fBjitter\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB1d\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR A random part of it, per run, is taken off the due time (and
given to \fBdnssec\-signzone \-j\fR), so many zones do not all fall due at the
same second.
.TP 8
\fBverify\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fByes\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBno\fR passes \fB\-P\fR to \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR: no
verification of the signed zone.
.TP 8
\fBnsec3\-opt\-out\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR NSEC3 Opt-Out (RFC 5155 section 6) for the zone's chain;
\fB\-A\fR. It applies from the next run.
.TP 8
\fBdebounce\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB5\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Seconds after the last change of a zone before it is signed
again; a burst of changes waits at most 6 times as long (30 s).
.TP 8
\fBtimeout\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB1800\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Seconds a child process may run (at least 10); then it is killed
and the zone fails.
.TP 8
\fBlog\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBstderr\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBstderr\fR, or a syslog facility (\fBdaemon\fR,
\fBlocal0\fR ... \fBlocal7\fR, ...): lines go to \fBlogger(1)\fR, tag
\fBkaridns\-signer\fR, priority \fBnotice\fR.
.PP
\fBvalidity\fR, \fBrefresh\fR, \fBjitter\fR, \fBverify\fR and
\fBnsec3\-opt\-out\fR can also be set per zone, in a \fB[zone <name>]\fR
section; the zone name is plain LDH (letters, digits, hyphen, underscore; IDNs
in their punycode form) and written once. The whole must satisfy \fBrefresh >
0\fR and \fB\fBrefresh + jitter < validity\fB\fR for the global values and for
every zone (otherwise every signature would fall due at once); \fB\-\-check\fR
and \fBSIGHUP\fR say which one does not.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
database = pg
conninfo\-file = /usr/local/etc/karidns/signer.conninfo
log = daemon
workers = 2
validity = 14d
refresh = 3d
jitter = 12h
\&
[zone example.test]
validity = 30d
refresh = 7d
jitter = 2d
\&
[zone optout.example.test]
nsec3\-opt\-out = yes
.fi
.RE
.SH SETUP
.IP 1. 4
\fBThe database\fR must be at schema version 3 with the signer's tables: apply
\fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR or \fBdb/karidns_ext.sqlite3.sql\fR
(\fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR, "The signer's tables"). PostgreSQL: \fBpsql \-v
ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-v role=karidns_signer \-v schema=public \-f
db/karidns_signer_role.pgsql.sql <db>\fR as the owner, then \fBGRANT
karidns_signer TO <login role>\fR (or create the role with \fBLOGIN\fR). The
script refuses a role that could bypass row level security.
.IP 2. 4
\fBThe signer's user.\fR The FreeBSD package \fBkaridns\-signer\fR (install one
of \fBkaridns\-sqlite3\fR, \fBkaridns\-postgresql\fR or \fBkaridns\-db\fR first:
it needs their \fBkaridns\-zone\fR) installs the program under
\fB/usr/local/libexec/karidns\-signer\fR with its \fBnode_modules\fR, a wrapper
\fB/usr/local/sbin/karidns\-signer\fR, the rc script \fBkaridns_signer\fR,
\fBkaridns\-signer.conf.sample\fR and the user and group \fBkaridns\-signer\fR;
it depends on \fBnode24\fR and \fBbind\-tools\fR (Distribution Guide, 2.6). The
signer must \fBnot\fR run as root, as the server's user or as the database
owner.
.IP 3. 4
\fBThe configuration file\fR and, for PostgreSQL, the conninfo file (\fBchown
karidns\-signer\fR, \fBchmod 0600\fR). Check it as the signer's user: \fBservice
karidns_signer configtest\fR, or \fBsu \-m karidns\-signer \-c 'karidns\-signer
\-\-check \-c /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns\-signer.conf'\fR. Before the first
start the work directory does not exist yet: configtest says so
(\fBwork\-dir ... does not exist yet: start makes it\fR) and \fB\-\-check\fR
reports it; configtest makes nothing.
.IP 4. 4
\fBThe server.\fR \fBdatabase { signer\-wait yes; }\fR is the default; see "The
server waits for the signer". With SQLite3 add the restricted control key of the
signer to the server (below).
.IP 5. 4
\fBStart:\fR \fBsysrc karidns_signer_enable=YES; service karidns_signer
start\fR. \fBThe work directory.\fR An existing \fBwork\-dir\fR is never
changed: it must be a directory of the signer's user with mode \fB0700\fR (what
\fB\-\-check\fR wants), else the start is refused. A missing one is made (the
user's, \fB0700\fR) only in an existing parent, or in
\fB/var/db/karidns\-signer\fR (the home of the package's user, the parent of the
default), which is made too; any other missing parent, a relative path, a
\fB.\fR or \fB..\fR component and a symbolic link in the path are refused (no
chain of directories is made as root). The rc script mounts a tmpfs
(\fBkaridns_signer_tmpfs_size\fR, default 256m, mode \fB0700\fR) on the
\fBwork\-dir\fR of the configuration file, starts the daemon with
\fBdaemon(8)\fR (restart after 10 s if it dies, for instance because the
database was down at boot), sets \fBulimit \-c 0\fR, and unmounts the tmpfs on
stop. Under the rc script standard error goes to /dev/null, so set \fBlog =
daemon\fR (or another facility) in the configuration file. \fBservice
karidns_signer reload\fR sends \fBSIGHUP\fR. \fBkaridns_signer_cpuset="2\-3"\fR
starts it under \fBcpuset \-l\fR.
.IP 6. 4
\fBPut a zone under the signer\fR (next section).
.SH KEY REQUESTS
A row in \fBkaridns_key_requests\fR asks the signer for something. The WebAdmin
files the rows (a global master or the zone's domain master); an operator can do
it with SQL:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
\-\- start signing example.test: a CSK, ECDSA P\-256, NSEC3 without salt and with 0 iterations (RFC 9276)
INSERT INTO karidns_key_requests (domain_id, kind)
  SELECT id, 'enable' FROM domains WHERE name = 'example.test';
\-\- more of it: KSK + ZSK instead of a CSK, ECDSA P\-384, NSEC instead of NSEC3
INSERT INTO karidns_key_requests (domain_id, kind, algorithm, key_type, nsec)
  SELECT id, 'enable', 14, 'ksk', 'nsec' FROM domains WHERE name = 'example.test';
\-\- the answer
SELECT r.id, d.name, r.kind, r.state, r.message FROM karidns_key_requests r JOIN domains d ON d.id = r.domain_id ORDER BY r.id DESC LIMIT 10;
.fi
.RE
.TP 8
\fBkind\fR
\fBenable\fR: start signing the zone. The signer generates the first key (a
\fBcsk\fR) or keys (\fBkey_type\fR \fBksk\fR or \fBzsk\fR makes a KSK \fBand\fR
a ZSK), signs the zone, stores the keys, and sets the metadata
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR, \fBPRESIGNED\fR and (NSEC3)
\fBNSEC3PARAM\fR. \fBadd\fR: one more key (\fBksk\fR or \fBzsk\fR; \fBcsk\fR
makes a key with the SEP bit, like \fBksk\fR) for a zone the signer signs; the
zone is signed again with it at once.
.TP 8
\fBalgorithm\fR
8 (RSASHA256), 10 (RSASHA512), 13 (ECDSAP256SHA256, the default), 14
(ECDSAP384SHA384), 15 (Ed25519), 16 (Ed448). Algorithms 5 and 7 (SHA-1) are
refused (RFC 8624).
.TP 8
\fBbits\fR
RSA only, 1024 to 8192.
.TP 8
\fBkey_type\fR
\fBcsk\fR (default; one key with the SEP bit signs everything), \fBksk\fR (flags
257) or \fBzsk\fR (flags 256).
.TP 8
\fBnsec\fR
\fBenable\fR only: \fBnsec3\fR (default) or \fBnsec\fR. A zone that is signed
already keeps its chain; the parameters are those of its \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR
metadata.
.TP 8
\fBstate\fR, \fBmessage\fR, \fBdone_at\fR
\fBpending\fR, then \fBdone\fR or \fBfailed\fR with the reason. One pending
request per zone: a second is refused until the first is answered.
.PP
The requests are polled every 5 seconds, 50 at a time; when a batch is started
in full, the next one is read at once, so a bulk of requests is not held to 10
zones per second. An \fBenable\fR request for a zone that has keys and the
metadata \fBPRESIGNED\fR already (for instance a zone PowerDNS signed, keys in
\fBcryptokeys\fR) generates \fBno\fR key: the signer takes over with the keys
that are there. A zone with keys but without \fBPRESIGNED\fR is refused, because
the server does not serve such a zone (no online signing).
.PP
\fBWhere the keys go.\fR \fBdnssec\-keygen\fR runs in the work directory; the
key is read from its \fB.private\fR file (Private-key-format v1.2, the form
PowerDNS keeps in \fBcryptokeys.content\fR), the files are wiped, the tag
computed by the signer is compared with the one \fBdnssec\-keygen\fR chose (RFC
4034 appendix B). The key is stored in \fBcryptokeys\fR (\fBflags\fR 257 or 256,
\fBactive\fR and \fBpublished\fR true) \fBafter\fR the signed zone is written,
so a key can never be in the database without a zone that uses it. If that
\fBINSERT\fR fails (database failover, say) the signer tries again for about 10
minutes (15 times, from 1 s to 60 s) with the same key; if it cannot, the
request is \fBfailed\fR with the message that the keys were lost and the DNSKEYs
have to be replaced with a request of kind \fBadd\fR.
.SH HOW A ZONE IS SIGNED
For each zone, in a work directory of its own (wiped on every path, also after
an error and at start):
.IP 1. 4
\fBLock.\fR PostgreSQL:
\fBpg_try_advisory_lock(hashtextextended('karidns\-signer:<id>', 0))\fR, so
several signer instances can share a database and a zone is signed by one of
them (a zone that another instance holds is tried again after 30 s). SQLite3:
one signer per database file (\fB<file>.signer.lock\fR).
.IP 2. 4
\fBKeys.\fR The \fBcryptokeys\fR rows with \fBactive\fR or \fBpublished\fR
become the DNSKEY RRs of the zone; the \fBactive\fR ones are written as key
files (\fB0600\fR) and sign. The zone needs an active key with the SEP bit
(flags 257), because the DS record at the parent is made from one.
.IP 3. 4
\fBExport.\fR \fBkaridns\-zone export \-\-signer\fR: the zone as the input of
\fBdnssec\-signzone\fR, without the NSEC / NSEC3 records (the database has none,
and a zone that was changed since it was signed has no complete chain) but with
all its signatures, those over the chain too (0.6.1), so that the ones that
still verify are kept. The DNSKEY lines are replaced by the ones of the keys.
.IP 4. 4
\fBSign.\fR \fBdnssec\-signzone \-q \-o <zone> \-O full \-e now+<validity> \-i
<refresh+jitter> \-j <jitter> \-N increment \-R \-u [\-3 \- \-H 0 [\-A]] [\-P]
(\-z | \-x)\fR with the key files of the \fBactive\fR keys. A CSK or a lone key
signs everything (\fB\-z\fR); with a KSK and a ZSK only the KSK signs the DNSKEY
RRset (\fB\-x\fR). The SOA serial is increased by one (\fB\-N
increment\fR). Signatures that have more than \fBrefresh + jitter\fR left are
kept (\fB\-i\fR), so a change does not re-sign the whole zone. \fB\-R\fR drops
the signatures of keys that are no longer in the zone. \fB\-u\fR (0.6.1) builds
the chain again from the parameters above (a new salt, opt-out or NSEC <-> NSEC3
takes effect; without \fB\-u\fR \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR refuses a chain with
another salt) and keeps every signature over it that still
verifies. \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR verifies the result unless \fB\-P\fR (\fBverify
no\fR).
.IP 5. 4
\fBCheck.\fR \fBkaricheck zone <zone> <signed file>\fR.
.IP 6. 4
\fBWrite.\fR \fBkaridns\-zone import \-\-signer \-\-json [\-\-reload]\fR in one
transaction: the DNSSEC rows are brought to the file's (0.6.1: only the rows
that differ are deleted and inserted; rows that are the same stay, with their
ids, and are not in the change log), the SOA serial updated, \fBordername\fR /
\fBauth\fR updated, the metadata set. If the zone changed since the export,
nothing is written (exit status 3) and the signer starts again at once (up to 3
times in a row, then it counts as an error). The result carries the new serial,
the \fBearliest RRSIG expiration\fR and the id of the write.
.IP 7. 4
\fBReload.\fR PostgreSQL: the row in \fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR is written by
\fBimport \-\-signer \-\-reload\fR in the same transaction as the
signatures. SQLite3: \fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR with the signer's restricted
key. A failed reload is a warning (the zone is signed; the server serves the old
version until it reloads).
.IP 8. 4
\fBState.\fR \fBkaridns_signer_state\fR: \fBsigned_at\fR, \fBsigned_serial\fR,
\fBmin_expire\fR, \fBnext_due\fR, \fBattempts\fR = 0, \fBlast_error\fR
NULL. Keys of an \fBenable\fR request are stored now (see above) and the request
is answered.
.PP
\fBDeviations from a plain \fBdnssec\-signzone\fB run, and why.\fR \fB\-i\fR is
\fBrefresh + jitter\fR (the run is placed up to \fBjitter\fR before the refresh
point). No timing metadata is written to the key files (\fB\-S\fR is not used)
and \fB\-Q\fR is not used (without timing metadata it would sign everything
again on every run). The DNSKEY RRs of all active and published keys are
appended to the input, because \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR 9.20 takes the keys from
the zone's DNSKEY RRset. Since 0.6.1 the NSEC / NSEC3 chain is built again in
every run, but the signatures over it that still verify are kept (the export
carries them, \fBdnssec\-signzone \-u\fR), and \fBimport \-\-signer\fR writes
only the DNSSEC rows that differ. Measured for one changed A record in a zone of
100,000 records (SQLite3, three runs each, median): the log of changed rows has
7 rows instead of 400,023, \fBdnssec\-verify\fR accepts the result, and the run
takes about 29 s instead of 31 s (CPU 37 s against 37 s; the importer needs 6 s
instead of 12 s, the export 1.3 s and \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR 3.5 s of CPU more,
and the export 290 MB more memory). So the gain is mostly in what is written and
what an IXFR and the audit log carry, not in time. A retired key (published, not
active) keeps the signatures it made over RRsets that did not change until they
expire; on a changed RRset its old signature (invalid, harmless) stays next to
the new one.
.PP
\fBOn error\fR nothing is written: the zone keeps its last good signed
data. \fBattempts\fR is increased, \fBlast_error\fR is set (cut to 1 KB, no key
material), and \fBnext_due\fR is now plus 1 minute, doubling to 1 hour. An
operator who has fixed the cause resets it with \fBUPDATE karidns_signer_state
SET next_due = <now>\fR (PostgreSQL: \fBnow()\fR, SQLite3:
\fBstrftime('%s','now')\fR); the signer sees it within a few seconds. A zone
whose \fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR metadata was removed is skipped and its state row
dropped.
.SH SCHEDULING
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBefore expiry.\fR \fBnext_due\fR = the earliest RRSIG expiration of the zone
\[u2212] \fBrefresh\fR \[u2212] a random part of \fBjitter\fR. (Not \fBsigned_at
+ validity \[u2212] refresh\fR: \fB\-i\fR keeps older signatures that still have
time, so some of them expire earlier than the new ones.) The due zones are read
in \fBnext_due\fR order with a limit (an index), so a million zones cost no full
scan. A signer zone without a state row is due at once.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOn change.\fR PostgreSQL: \fBLISTEN karidns_changes\fR (and a look at the
change log every 30 s as a safety net); SQLite3: a poll of \fBkaridns_changes\fR
every 2 s. A change to a signer zone makes it due \fBdebounce\fR seconds after
the last change. The signer's own writes (the id the importer reports: the
transaction id on PostgreSQL, the range of \fBseq\fR on SQLite3) are not
changes; a record merged in without a serial bump is. A change that the signer
had read when a run of the zone started (the signer reads the change log before
it starts a run; a key request right after the zone was made) is covered by that
run when it signs the zone: it is not signed a second time for it; a run that
does not sign the zone (busy, locked, skipped, failed) leaves the change to make
the zone due.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAt start and every hour\fR a catch-up scan makes due the zones that have no
state row, that changed after their last signature, or whose SOA serial is not
the one the signer wrote. A change log that was pruned beyond the signer's
position triggers the same scan.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWorkers.\fR \fBworkers\fR zones are signed at a time. A database that is busy
(exit status 4 of \fBkaridns\-zone\fR, or a PostgreSQL write that was rolled
back for a concurrent update: \fBcould not serialize access\fR, \fBdeadlock
detected\fR) is tried again after 5 s, doubling to 1 minute; nothing was
written.
.SH THE SERVER WAITS FOR THE SIGNER
A change to a signed zone (by the WebAdmin, a script, \fBkaridns\-zone
import\fR) puts records into the database that have no valid signature yet. A
validating resolver must never see a changed RRset with a stale signature, so
with \fBdatabase { signer\-wait yes; }\fR (the default; \fBkaridns(8)\fR, "The
signer's zones") the loader \fBdoes not reload a zone with
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fB = 1 because of the change log\fR. It reloads on a
reload request: the signer's after it has written the signatures. Until then the
server serves the last signed version. Consequences:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLatency.\fR A change is served \fBdebounce\fR + the signing time + the reload
later. The signing time of a small zone is a fraction of a second; see the
measurements.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBA stopped or failing signer\fR keeps changes from being served. \fBkarictl
dbstatus\fR shows \fBwaiting for the signer: N zone(s): ...\fR; a count that
does not go down means the signer is not working. \fBsigner\-wait no;\fR serves
changes at once, unsigned.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNot held back:\fR a zone the server does not have yet, a full comparison
(start, restart, \fBkarictl reload\fR of every zone, a replaced database file)
and a refused zone. In those moments an unsigned change can be served; the
signer catches up within seconds.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPowerDNS\fR on the same database reads it live and serves a change at once,
signed or not. Keep it apart from the zones the signer signs.
.SH DATABASE ROLES, AND RELOADS WITH SQLITE3
\fBPostgreSQL.\fR The signer connects as the role of
\fBdb/karidns_signer_role.pgsql.sql\fR (row level security on). It reads the
tables and \fBcryptokeys.content\fR and the change log; inserts and deletes the
DNSSEC record types; updates \fBordername\fR, \fBauth\fR and the SOA serial (a
trigger holds it to that); writes the three metadata kinds; \fBinserts\fR keys
(no update, no delete); answers key requests (\fBstate\fR, \fBmessage\fR,
\fBdone_at\fR); owns \fBkaridns_signer_state\fR; inserts reload requests. It
cannot write an A record, change a key or touch the users and grants. The
WebAdmin's role files key requests and reads the state table; it cannot read
\fBcryptokeys.content\fR. Details: \fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR, "Roles and row
level security". PostgreSQL 13 or newer.
.PP
\fBSQLite3.\fR There are no roles: the file is opened by the signer's user (and
by the server's loader and the API), so keep the file and its directory in a
group both can write, or run the loader's user and the signer under one account
of your choosing. SQLite3 has no reload requests, so the signer asks the server
over the control channel with \fBkarictl\fR, with a key that can do \fBnothing
but \fBreload\-zone\fB\fR:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
# karidns.conf
key "signer" { algorithm hmac\-sha256; secret "c2lnbmVyLXNlY3JldC0wMTIzNDU2Nzg5YWJjZGVmMDEyMw=="; };   # a secret of your own
options {
    control\-channel {
        socket "/var/run/karidns/karidns.sock";
        keys { "signer" read\-only yes allow\-commands { reload\-zone; }; };
    };
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
# /usr/local/etc/karidns/signer\-karictl.conf (owner karidns\-signer, mode 0600); karictl\-config points here
socket "/var/run/karidns/karidns.sock";
key "signer" { algorithm hmac\-sha256; secret "c2lnbmVyLXNlY3JldC0wMTIzNDU2Nzg5YWJjZGVmMDEyMw=="; };
.fi
.RE
.PP
The key may reload one named zone (\fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR), never all, and
can read nothing else of the server (\fBkaridns(8)\fR, "Restricted control
keys"). The control socket must be reachable by the signer's user.
.SH SECURITY
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNo shell.\fR Every child process is \fBspawn(file, argv)\fR with absolute
paths, a fixed environment (\fBPATH\fR, \fBLANG=C\fR, \fBLC_ALL=C\fR,
\fBTZ=UTC\fR, \fBHOME\fR, and \fBLLVM_PROFILE_FILE\fR when it is set, which only
a coverage build reads), a timeout and a cap of 1 MB on each output stream.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZone names\fR are checked (canonical, plain LDH, no leading hyphen) before
they reach a file name or an argv word.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBKey material\fR exists only in the database, in the \fB0700\fR work directory
(on tmpfs) for the time a zone is signed, and in the memory of
\fBdnssec\-signzone\fR. The files are overwritten and unlinked after each zone
and on every error path; leftovers of a crash (\fBkill \-9\fR) are wiped at the
next start. The rc script and the wrapper set \fBulimit \-c 0\fR. Keys and key
text are never logged; messages of the tools are cut to 1 KB.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPrivilege.\fR Its own user, no root. The database role cannot write user data
(above). The conninfo file is checked like the loader's.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe one npm dependency\fR is \fBpg\fR with an exact version and an integrity
hash in \fBpackage\-lock.json\fR; install scripts are off
(\fB\-\-ignore\-scripts\fR), CI runs \fBnpm audit \-\-audit\-level=high\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe tools.\fR BIND's \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR parses the exported zone, which
is data from your own database; it runs in the work directory as the signer's
user.
.SH MONITORING
Look at \fBkaridns_signer_monitor\fR (0.6.1; apply the new
\fBdb/karidns_ext.*.sql\fR again to get the view). It has one row per zone the
signer keeps (\fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER = 1\fR) with its \fBkaridns_signer_state\fR
columns and a \fBstatus\fR, the first that applies of \fBexpired\fR (the
earliest RRSIG expiration, \fBmin_expire\fR, has passed: validating resolvers
refuse the zone now), \fBfailing\fR (\fBlast_error\fR is set), \fBoverdue\fR
(\fBnext_due\fR is more than an hour in the past: the signer is stuck or not
running), \fBexpiring\fR (the signatures end within two days; the signer
re-signs long before that, so this and not \fBoverdue\fR means it fails to
sign), \fBunsigned\fR (no state row yet: never signed, or the signer is not
running) and \fBok\fR. \fBseconds_to_expiry\fR is \fBmin_expire \- now\fR
(negative when expired). \fBA zone whose signatures run out is the failure to
alert on\fR: the server serves signatures that validating resolvers reject once
they expire (SERVFAIL for the whole zone). Alert on every \fBstatus\fR but
\fBok\fR.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
SELECT zone, status, seconds_to_expiry, attempts, last_error, signed_at, min_expire, next_due FROM karidns_signer_monitor WHERE status <> 'ok' ORDER BY seconds_to_expiry;
.fi
.RE
.PP
PostgreSQL: the view belongs to the owner; give a monitoring role \fBGRANT
SELECT ON karidns_signer_monitor TO monitor;\fR and nothing else (the view does
not apply the reader's row level security, so that role sees every signer zone,
and it cannot read \fBcryptokeys\fR). SQLite3: the times are unix seconds, as in
the table. The thresholds (one hour, two days) are fixed in the view; for
others, query \fBkaridns_signer_state\fR directly:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
\-\- PostgreSQL: zones with an error, zones overdue, zones whose signatures end within two days
SELECT d.name, s.attempts, s.last_error, s.signed_at, s.min_expire, s.next_due
  FROM karidns_signer_state s JOIN domains d ON d.id = s.domain_id
 WHERE s.last_error IS NOT NULL OR s.next_due < now() \- interval '1 hour' OR s.min_expire < now() + interval '2 days';
\-\- SQLite3: the same with unix seconds
SELECT d.name, s.attempts, s.last_error, s.signed_at, s.min_expire, s.next_due
  FROM karidns_signer_state s JOIN domains d ON d.id = s.domain_id
 WHERE s.last_error IS NOT NULL OR s.next_due < strftime('%s','now') \- 3600 OR s.min_expire < strftime('%s','now') + 172800;
\-\- signer zones that have no state row (not signed yet, or the signer is not running)
SELECT d.name FROM domains d JOIN domainmetadata m ON m.domain_id = d.id AND m.kind = 'X\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER' AND m.content = '1'
 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM karidns_signer_state s WHERE s.domain_id = d.id);
.fi
.RE
.PP
Also watch: \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR (zones waiting for the signer), the requests
with \fBstate = 'failed'\fR or \fBpending\fR for a long time, the daemon's log
(\fBsigned <zone>: serial N, M ms\fR; errors as \fB<zone>: ...\fR), and an
external check of the served zone (\fBdag @server example.test SOA +dnssec\fR
and \fBdnssec\-verify\fR on an export). The signing time of the last run is in
\fBlast_duration_ms\fR.
.SH KEY ROLLOVER
The signer does not roll keys by itself; it follows the columns \fBactive\fR and
\fBpublished\fR of \fBcryptokeys\fR: the DNSKEY RRset is made of the rows with
either set, and the \fBactive\fR keys sign. The operator does the rollover with
the timing of RFC 7583 (section 3 defines the intervals; section 4 the
methods). Take the TTLs from your zone and the parent: \fBthe DNSKEY TTL, the
largest TTL of the signed RRsets, the DS TTL at the parent, the propagation
delay between your servers, and the signature \fBvalidity\fB\fR. Use a database
owner session; the signer's role cannot update or delete keys.
.PP
\fBZSK, pre-publish (RFC 7583 section 4).\fR The new key is published before it
signs.
.IP 1. 4
Make a key with \fBdnssec\-keygen \-a 13 \-K <dir> example.test\fR (a ZSK; flags
256). Insert it \fBpublished, not active\fR: \fBINSERT INTO cryptokeys
(domain_id, flags, active, published, content) VALUES (<id>, 256, false,
true, '<the text of the .private file>');\fR. The insert is in the change log,
the signer signs the zone again (the DNSKEY RRset now has the new key and is
signed). \fIA request of kind \f(BIadd\fI makes a key that is active at once;
that is a double-signature rollover, also valid, but not this one.\fR
.IP 2. 4
Wait at least the DNSKEY TTL plus the propagation delay.
.IP 3. 4
Switch: \fBUPDATE cryptokeys SET active = true WHERE id = <new>; UPDATE
cryptokeys SET active = false WHERE id = <old>;\fR. The zone is signed again:
the new key signs every RRset at once, and the signatures of the old key stay
next to them until they expire (every RRset carries both for now; an RRset that
changes later is signed by the new key only). The old key stays published.
.IP 4. 4
Wait the largest TTL of the zone plus the propagation delay, so that every cache
has seen the new signatures.
.IP 5. 4
Retire: \fBDELETE FROM cryptokeys WHERE id = <old>;\fR. The signer drops the old
DNSKEY RR and the signatures of the old key (\fB\-R\fR) and nothing else
changes.
.PP
Every step makes the signer sign the zone again (a few hundred milliseconds for
a small zone); \fBdnssec\-verify\fR accepts the zone after each of them (checked
on pc200 with a KSK, two ZSKs and a small zone).
.PP
\fBKSK, double-DS (RFC 7583 section 4).\fR With a CSK the same applies (a CSK is
a KSK).
.IP 1. 4
File a request \fBadd\fR with \fBkey_type\fR \fBksk\fR (or insert a key with
flags 257). The new KSK is active and published; both KSKs sign the DNSKEY
RRset. (When the old key is a CSK, both keys sign the whole zone until the old
one is retired, a double signature.)
.IP 2. 4
Compute the DS of the new key (\fBdag @server example.test DNSKEY +short\fR,
then \fBdnssec\-dsfromkey \-f <file> example.test\fR, or from the \fB.key\fR
text) and add it at the parent. Wait until the parent serves it and its TTL has
passed.
.IP 3. 4
Remove the old DS at the parent. Wait the DS TTL and the propagation delay.
.IP 4. 4
Stop signing with the old key: \fBUPDATE cryptokeys SET active = false WHERE id
= <old>\fR; wait the DNSKEY TTL; \fBDELETE FROM cryptokeys WHERE id = <old>\fR.
.PP
Algorithm rollovers (RFC 6781) follow the same steps with the two algorithms'
keys both present; plan them by hand. Never delete a key whose DS is still at
the parent.
.SH LIMITS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe first signing of a zone is also the longest\fR; the NSEC / NSEC3 chain is
rebuilt on every run (above), so a re-sign of a big zone costs nearly as much
CPU as the first signing (the measurements show the factor), while the
signatures that are still good are kept.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCatch-up limit.\fR A change by someone else without a serial bump within
about 1 to 2 seconds of the last signature is found only at the next change of
the zone or at expiry, not by the change log (it cannot be told from the
signer's own rows by time). The hourly scan compares the SOA serial and finds
the usual cases. On PostgreSQL a long-running transaction elsewhere (an old
\fBxmin\fR) can leave a signed zone listed as waiting until its next reload.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRetired keys\fR keep their old signatures until they expire (above).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSQLite3: a row per changed row.\fR Its triggers write one change-log row per
changed row (PostgreSQL: a few per write), and the signer reads 20,000 rows at a
time (a full batch is followed by the next read at once, 0.6.1; before that, one
batch per 2-s poll). The rows of its own write for a zone of 100,000 records are
skipped over in a few seconds; a change made by someone else in that time is
seen after it. Memory: a run of \fBkaridns\-zone import \-\-signer\fR for
100,000 records peaks at 640 to 750 MB (see the measurements); with big zones
keep \fBworkers\fR low.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAlgorithms\fR 8, 10, 13, 14, 15, 16. Algorithm 5 and 7 are refused. A zone
name must be plain LDH.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZone types.\fR Only primary zones (\fBNATIVE\fR, \fBMASTER\fR) with one SOA
at the apex.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOne signer per SQLite3 file\fR; several on PostgreSQL (advisory locks).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNot in this release:\fR automated rollover, CDS / CDNSKEY, online signing,
DNSSEC validation, signing with an HSM.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNSEC or NSEC3\fR is chosen by the request that starts the signing; a signed
zone keeps the setting in its \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR metadata (\fBnsec3\-opt\-out\fR
is read at every run). There is no request to change it.
.SH RUNNING IT ELSEWHERE
The signer never competes with the server for CPU if you do not let it. Signing
is CPU heavy for a short time (a big zone, many zones due together),
\fBdnssec\-signzone\fR is single threaded, and \fBworkers\fR multiplies it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSame host:\fR give the signer CPUs of its own and keep \fBworkers\fR no
larger than their number, for example \fBkaridns_signer_cpuset="3"\fR in
\fBrc.conf\fR (\fBcpuset \-l 3\fR) or \fBcpuset \-l 3 \-p
<pid>\fR. \fBkaridns\fR does not pin its threads (since 0.6.1), so it uses
whatever CPUs are free; on a host with few CPUs, leave most of them to the
server (on 4 CPUs at most one or two for the signer). \fBrtprio\fR / \fBnice\fR
do not matter for a CPU set of its own.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOther host:\fR the signer needs only the database (and, with SQLite3, the
file and the server's control socket, which is why PostgreSQL is the choice for
this), \fBkaridns\-zone\fR, \fBkaricheck\fR, \fBbind\-tools\fR and Node. Install
the same package on a host that runs no server, enable only
\fBkaridns_signer\fR, and use a conninfo file that names the database host
(\fBsslmode=verify\-full\fR). The signed data reaches the servers through the
database; their loaders reload on the reload requests.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSeveral instances\fR on one PostgreSQL database share the zones (advisory
locks). They need the same configuration.
.SH MEASUREMENTS
Measured on the build host of the project on 2026-11-06, with the defaults of
this manual (ECDSA P-256 CSK, NSEC3 without salt and with 0 iterations,
\fBvalidity\fR 14d, \fBrefresh\fR 3.5 days, \fBjitter\fR 1 day, \fBverify
yes\fR), \fBlog = stderr\fR, the work directory on the ordinary disk (the jail
has no tmpfs), and no server: the SQLite3 reload (\fBkarictl reload\fR) fails
fast on every zone (no socket), so each run includes one failed
\fBkarictl\fR. The numbers are the host's, not a guarantee.
.PP
