\fB/usr/local/libexec/karidns\-signer/\fR
The program (\fBsigner/bin/\fR, \fBsigner/lib/\fR), the Node code it shares with
\fBkaridns\-webadmin\fR (\fBnode/lib/\fR, imported as \fB../../node/lib\fR) and
its \fBnode_modules\fR (\fBpg\fR). \fBroot:wheel\fR, not writable.
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/etc/rc.d/karidns_signer\fR
The rc script (\fBkaridns_signer_enable\fR, \fB_config\fR, \fB_user\fR,
\fB_tmpfs\fR, \fB_tmpfs_size\fR, \fB_cpuset\fR, \fB_flags\fR; commands
\fBstart\fR, \fBstop\fR, \fBrestart\fR, \fBstatus\fR, \fBreload\fR,
\fBconfigtest\fR).
.TP 8
\fB/var/db/karidns\-signer/work\fR
The default work directory (tmpfs, \fB0700\fR, owned by \fBkaridns\-signer\fR).
.TP 8
\fB/var/run/karidns_signer/\fR
The pid files of \fBdaemon(8)\fR (\fBsupervisor.pid\fR for \fBservice
karidns_signer stop\fR, \fBchild.pid\fR for \fBreload\fR); the directory belongs
to the signer's user because the rc script runs \fBdaemon\fR as that user
(rc.subr does it with \fBsu \-m\fR, for \fBkaridns_signer_user\fR), so
\fBdaemon\fR writes them as that user.
.TP 8
\fB<sqlite\-file>.signer.lock\fR
The lock file of a SQLite3 database (the pid of the signer).
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_signer_role.pgsql.sql\fR
The privileges of the PostgreSQL role (\fBdb/\fR in a source tree).
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a configuration as the signer's user, then start the daemon in the
foreground with the log on the terminal:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
su \-m karidns\-signer \-c 'karidns\-signer \-\-check \-c /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns\-signer.conf'
su \-m karidns\-signer \-c 'karidns\-signer \-c /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns\-signer.conf'      # log = stderr in that file
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sign a new zone and look at the answer:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-zone import \-c /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf example.test example.test.zone
psql \-c "INSERT INTO karidns_key_requests (domain_id, kind) SELECT id, 'enable' FROM domains WHERE name = 'example.test'" pdns
psql \-c "SELECT state, message FROM karidns_key_requests ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 1" pdns
dag @192.0.2.53 example.test DNSKEY +dnssec +nohexdump
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check the signed zone of the database from outside:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-zone export \-c /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf example.test example.test.signed
dnssec\-verify \-z \-o example.test example.test.signed      # \-z: a CSK (one key with the SEP bit signs everything) is not a "missing ZSK"; leave \-z out for a KSK and a ZSK
.fi
.RE
